@page "/components/navs"

<DocPage Title="Navs">
	<Interjection>
		<strong>Be aware:</strong> TorchUI separates navs and tabs into separate components because tabs have layout and functionality which navs do not. The TorchUI <code>&lt;Nav&gt;</code> component <em>does</em> have the option to render as a tab list, but this is presentational only. If you want a tab list, use the <code>&lt;TabList&gt;</code> component.
	</Interjection>

	<Interjection
		Color="Warning"
		OmitWrapper>
		<p>
			<strong>Be aware:</strong> TorchUI has a component named <code>&lt;NavLink&gt;</code>, which conflicts with the built-in <code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Routing.NavLink</code> component. While this is generally bad manners, we did this for two reasons:
		</p>

		<ol>
			<li>
				TorchUI generally names components after the CSS class used to style them
			</li>
			<li>
				Users of TorchUI.Bootstrap are probably all-in on Bootstrap, meaning the TorchUI-provided <code>&lt;NavLink&gt;</code> is what they will use most, if not all, of the time
			</li>
		</ol>

		<p>
			Unfortunately, the Razor template compiler doesn't currently support type aliases, so this naming collision can't be circumvented. To avoid the issue altogether, we recommend that you take the following steps:
		</p>

		<ul>
			<li>
				Omit <code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Routing</code> from your <code>_imports.razor</code> file and only import it where actually needed, such as in <code>Routes.razor</code>
			</li>
			<li>
				If you need to use the <code>NavLinkMatch</code> enum, import it statically, i.e. <code>@@using static Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Routing.NavLinkMatch</code>. This has the added effect of importing only that enum from its namespace
			</li>
			<li>
				If you need to use the built-in <code>&lt;NavLink&gt;</code> component, you can use the <code>&lt;BlazorNavLink&gt;</code> component, which is a simple wrapper around the built-in <code>&lt;NavLink&gt;</code> component
			</li>
		</ul>
	</Interjection>

